  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Air is being pumped into a rubber speed boat. The rate at which the pressure increases in the boat is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2t+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in PSI per second and $t>0$ is the time in seconds since the pumping started.\n\nThe boat has a slow puncture which releases air at a rate of $1$ PSI every second.\n\nIf the boat had a pressure of $10$ PSI in the beginning, how much pressure does it have after $20$ seconds?",
  "worked_solution": "The pressure increases at a rate of $2t+4$ PSI per second and is decreasing at a rate of $1$ PSI per second. Therefore, the **rate of change of pressure** (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-1 \\\\  & =2t+4-1 \\\\  & =2t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total pressure** after $t$ seconds (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the quantity of pressure after $20$ seconds, we substitute $t=20$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(20) & =10+{\\int }_{0}^{20}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+{[{x}^{2}+3x]|}_{0}^{20} \\\\  & =10+[({20}^{2}+3(20))] \\\\  & =10+460 \\\\  & =470\\end{aligned}\n$$",
